	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105021130526&doi=10.1109%2FACCESS.2025.3629760&partnerID=40&md5=86e6e2e682a9420b90f75d465501e33f},
	affiliations = {UNSW Sydney, Transitional Artificial Intelligence Research Group, Sydney, NSW, Australia},
	abstract = {Over the past few decades, IMDb (Internet Movie Database) has become prominent internationally for rating and user-generated reviews of movies. However, there has not been much research that evaluates the quality of user-generated reviews and their associated ratings. In this study, we present a comprehensive deep learning-based framework that integrates movie rating prediction, abuse detection, sentiment analysis, and aspect extraction to analyse IMDb movie reviews across different rating tiers. We extracted and curated 137,886 reviews from 8,582 movies and employed pre-trained GloVe embeddings to obtain word vectors for high-frequency vocabulary, facilitating an in-depth examination of how linguistic features vary across ratings. Our findings indicate that higher-rated reviews predominantly express positive sentiment, whereas lower-rated reviews exhibit a higher prevalence of abusive language. We further examine cross-cultural differences among reviewers of different nationalities writing in English, revealing variations in sentiment intensity and expression. Using a language model framework, we offer insights into how cultural context shapes English-language movie reviews. Our results show that even high-rated reviews can exhibit strong abusive language, while low-rated reviews can contain positive sentiments. Across all rating levels, re-watchability consistently emerges as a central theme, highlighting the nuanced and complex nature of user-generated movie reviews. © 2013 IEEE.},
