	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105020503110&doi=10.1201%2F9781003593034-48&partnerID=40&md5=6a4dd5cc947bf00279289388389b76cf},
	affiliations = {Meerut Institute of Engineering & Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Meerut, UP, India},
	abstract = {Social media provides probably the most profound segregation possible through networking, at the same time offering the worst kind of hatred and poisonous content possible, threatening unity. Common methods of hate speech identification such as logistic regression, decision trees, and rules-based heuristics fail to address the complexity of the code-mixed data, different languages used in the text, as well as the context in which the text was written. But even complex machine learning is unable to generalize over multilingual corpora and does not capture cultural and contextual semiotics. Presenting this analysis shows the necessity of a strong, context-savvy model that rapidly identifies communal hate speech in multiple lingo for most of the platforms. Nowadays solutions like transformer encoders, small and large, sentiment enriched neural networks, and attention models are showing to be a good starting point to address these issues. Hence normalization and data augmentation that acts as tokenization or pre-processing methods helps to enhance the model resilience and handle issues related to imbalanced labels. A dataset collection including social media posts that are written in multiple languages or English and non-English words, comments from English articles and news articles reflect the idea that these techniques are quite suitable for multilingual code-switching. There are still major challenges like enlarging the scalability of the chosen model, improving the generalization for low-resource languages, and providing real-time detection. Hate speech ethics, interpretability, and dynamism present further difficulties in solving the problem. These deficiencies are discussed in this paper, and you will discover that context-aware deep learning methodologies are promising approaches to fill these gaps and provide significant improvement over traditional methods in classifying the communal hatred speech. Improving online safety and developing multilingual sentiment analysis, these extensions create a foundation for inventions meant to reduce hate speech while improving digital environments for all. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Pushpa Choudhary, Sambit Satpathy, Arvind Dagur and Dhirendra Kumar Shukla; individual chapters, the contributors},
