	abstract = {The word "misogyny"is derived from the ancient Greek word "misogunía,"which denotes a strong dislike for women. Social media services are increasingly being abused by others to disseminate unpleasant and abusive information, in addition to providing people with a forum to express their ideas, emotions and opinions. Detecting such content is particularly complex, as the meaning conveyed by images and overlaid text often lacks a direct correlation. This ambiguity increases the difficulty of automated detection, especially when memes propagate derogatory or hateful messages targeting women. A route to more effective and efficient model training is provided by Semi Supervised Learning (SSL) methods, particularly in situations when labeled data is limited. To facilitate the automatic detection of misogynistic sentiment within these comments, SSL algorithms such as self training, co-training and mean student-teacher models are explored with MAMI dataset contains 10000 memes and results are compared. This work contributes to the ongoing efforts in automatic hate speech detection and presents a valuable dataset for further advancements in multimodal meme detecting research. © 2025 IEEE.},
