	affiliations = {University College of Engineering, Hyderabad, Department of Electrical & Computer Engineering, Hyderabad, TS, India; Incline Inventions Pvt. Ltd, Hyderabad, India},
	abstract = {The dependence of the digital era on video content across various platforms continues to underscore the immense need for surveillance systems to detect violence and protect people. However, existing methods find it hard to handle the complexities of video analysis, given that newer methods are required to detect violent behaviors in massive video data. Thus, the current research seeks to address the existing gap in automatic violence detection by presenting a novel deep learning-based method. This paper presents a 3D convolutional ResNet18 model, which is utilized for feature extraction and enable improved spatiotemporal patterns for better classification. The current methodology used the ResNet18 3D model as a backbone to extract features, using dropout, and activation to enhance nonlinear features during pre-processing to avoid overfitting. A fully connected layer was then applied to reduce the feature space and followed by a binary output layer for classification. The model is highly effective as it can identify complex patterns in video and achieve quick and accurate detection of violent actions. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2024.},
