	affiliations = {University of Mauritius, Reduit, Mauritius; INTI International University, Faculty of Data Science and Information Technology, Nilai, Negeri Sembilan, Malaysia; University of Technology, Mauritius, Port Louis, Port Louis, Mauritius; Universitas Bina Darma, Postgraduate Program, Palembang, South Sumatra, Indonesia},
	abstract = {Social media platforms have become breeding grounds for abusive comments, necessitating the use of machine learning to detect harmful content. This study aims to predict abusive comments within a Mauritian context, focusing specifically on comments written in Mauritian Kreol, a language with limited natural language processing tools. The objective was to build and evaluate four machine learning models—Decision Tree, Random Forest, Naïve Bayes, and Support Vector Machine (SVM)—to accurately classify comments as abusive or non-abusive. The models were trained and tested using k-fold cross-validation, and the Decision Tree model outperformed others with 100% precision and recall, while Random Forest followed with 99% accuracy. Naïve Bayes and SVM, although achieving 100% precision, had lower recall rates of 35% and 16%, respectively, due to imbalanced data in the training set. Pre-processing steps, including stop-word removal and a custom Kreol spell checker, were key in enhancing model performance. The study provides a novel contribution by applying machine learning in a Mauritian context, demonstrating the potential of AI in detecting abusive language in underrepresented languages. Despite limitations such as the absence of a Kreol lemmatization tool and incomplete coverage of Kreol spelling variations, the models show promise for wider application in social media crime detection. Future research could explore expanding this approach to other languages and domains of social media crimes. © Authors retain all copyrights.},
