	affiliations = {Bina Nusantara University, Department of Psychology, Jakarta, Indonesia},
	abstract = {The study examines exploring the nexus: Loneliness in cyberbullying, and problematic internet use in modern social media dynamics. The study applied quantitative method with a total of 121 participants aged 18-25 in Indonesia. Feeling of loneliness were measured using the Social and Emotional Loneliness Scale for Adults (SELSA). Problematic internet use (PIU) was measured using the Generalized Problematic Internet Use Scale 2 (GPIUS2). Both SELSA and GPIUS2 adapted to Indonesian language. Cyberbullying behavior was measured using a cyberbullying scale based on cyberbullying theory. The results of the analysis are: 1) emotional loneliness does not have a direct effect on cyberbullying behavior, 2) social loneliness has a significant direct effect on cyberbullying behavior, and 3) social loneliness also has a significant indirect effect on cyberbullying behavior through the mediation of problematic internet use. Thus, it can be said that loneliness, especially social loneliness, plays a role in predicting cyberbullying behavior with the mediation of problematic internet use. © 2024 IEEE.},
