	affiliations = {Birla Institute of Technology and Science, Pilani – Dubai Campus, Dubai, United Arab Emirates; Birla Institute of Technology and Science, Pilani – Dubai Campus, Dubai, United Arab Emirates},
	abstract = {Over recent years, the prevalence of hate speech and provocative language on social media platforms has risen sharply, raising significant concerns about their impact on individuals and society at large. Hate speech can inflict harm on both individuals and groups, deepen societal divisions, and even incite violent acts. Therefore, the objective of the paper is to identify and eliminate hate speech from social media to help maintain a respectful and healthy online environment. The proposed work analyzes the BERT Model that has been fine-tuned by utilizing the Hugging Face library, to detect hate speech, offensive language, and neutral content. The aim of the research is to evaluate the model's accuracy and other performance metrics in identifying hate speech and offensive language. For training and testing, we employ a labeled dataset from Kaggle containing instances of offensive and hateful language. The BERT model uses a pre-trained tokenizer from the Hugging Face Transformers library, specifically the case-sensitive base BERT model, to tokenize and encode text for classification purposes. The model achieves an overall accuracy of 9 0%. © 2024 IEEE.},
