	doi = {10.1109/KSE63888.2024.11063520},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105011593274&doi=10.1109%2FKSE63888.2024.11063520&partnerID=40&md5=5e858b2fc29a0e85f199df65db4f1bae},
	affiliations = {Universiti Teknologi MARA, Informatics and Mathematics, Shah Alam, Selangor, Malaysia; Universiti Kebangsaan Malaysia, Faculty of Information Science and Technology, Bangi, Selangor, Malaysia},
	abstract = {While social media facilitates fast communication and rapid information exchange, it may also be used to launch harmful campaigns against certain organizations, communities, and individuals. Any kind of communication, spoken, written, that expresses prejudice, hostility, or violence towards a specific person or group is referred to as hateful posts or hate speech. Hate speech discriminates against people or groups by antagonistic behavior such as Anti-Black, Anti Muslim, Anti-Asian, xenophobia, misogyny, and homophobia. The introduction of deep learning and transformer has marked a significant turning point and offered models capable of understanding complex language patterns of hate speech. Thus, this paper reviews the progress of hybrid deep learning models and hybrid transformer models for hate speech detection through database resources and citation searches. The study was carried out methodically by analyzing 136 collections of screened articles about the detection and classification of hate speech that used single deep learning and hybrid deep learning to classify hate speech data. The articles are searched from publication in the year 2017 to 2024. As a result, about 40 articles have been selected for review based on the inclusion and exclusion criteria. The findings included the single hybrid learning models, multitask learning, multichannel learning and ensemble learning on English language and low resource languages. The challenges of using a hybrid approaches are mainly due to the complexity of multi-components to improve the contextual of hate speech data in sequence models on monolingual, multilingual which exist in various domain of hate speech. © 2024 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {BERT; deep learning; hate speech detection; hybrid deep learning; transformer model},
