	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105000743091&doi=10.1109%2FICAC2N63387.2024.10895953&partnerID=40&md5=f1136ea03f25898ed93641da0ab1e185},
	affiliations = {Galgotias University, School of Computing Science and Engineering, Greater Noida, UP, India},
	abstract = {Global communication has seen significant changes with the advent of social media platforms, one of which is a jump in improper conduct, such as the use of derogatory and violent words online. Finding such stuff is essential for banning or filtering objectionable information from the Internet. However, automated approaches are crucial for recognising this kind of information because of the massive volume of data produced daily. In an effort to tackle this problem, the study of natural language processing is investigating a growing number of methods for identifying hate speech. These methods show promise; however, they only focus on hate speech identification as the optimisation goal, leaving out associated tasks like polarity and emotion categorization that are closely related to rude actions. This study presents the first multitask method, based on a wellknown Transformer-based model, that uses emotional data supplied by users to identify hate speech in Spanish tweets. According to my findings, it is possible to identify hate speech more reliably across datasets when polarity and emotional knowledge are combined. © 2024 IEEE.},
