	affiliations = {Amity University Madhya Pradesh, Gwalior, MP, India},
	abstract = {We have been accustomed to the Internet being a readily available medium, so it comes as no surprise that there are numerous online platforms available to us. Increasing their use has benefits as well as drawbacks. Hate speech represents one such drawback. Online media platforms are concerned about hate speech. Manual post mediation is either completely unfeasible or extremely laborious with rapidly growing datasets. The objective of automated hate speech identification should be to separate hate speech from input. One kind of neural network model that is presented in our research is the multiple channel recurrent neural network system. Deep learning-focused implementation is made. The three outputs generated by the multiple layers and components of the Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) model are provocative language, hate and non-hate speech. After every channel is integrated and connected to a completely connected layer, the desired result is gathered. MCRNN performed superior to a simple We saw the performance of the RNN when we compared our model to a single-channel convolutional neural network. For D1 dataset, our model's accuracy and F1 score were 95.59 and 94.03 percentage and the accuracy and F1 score for D2 dataset were 95.84 and 97.95 percentage respectively. © 2024 IEEE.},
