	abstract = {This research article presents an investigation into a hate speech dataset that contains 2,478 tweets. The dataset was meticulously annotated by human annotators, and it includes two types of labels: hate (45.56%) and not-hate. We elaborate on the annotation process and the inter-annotator agreement attained. Additionally, we carry out a thorough analysis of the dataset, which encompasses exploratory data analysis, statistical analysis, and machine learning experiments. Our results demonstrate that recognizing hate speech is a difficult task due to its highly subjective nature among annotators. However, our dataset can be an advantageous resource for training and assessing hate speech detection models. We also discuss the ethical considerations that must be addressed while collecting and using hate speech data and provide recommendations for future research in this field. © 2023 IEEE.},
